  "worked_solution": "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to calculate the velocity function. We can do that by taking the derivative of the position function, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe particle is stationary when $v=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$ So, we set $v=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8t-7}{2\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}-7t+6}} & =0 \\\\ 8t-7 & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particle is stationary at $t=\\frac{7}{8}\\,\\text{s}.$",
